# Encryption Design – Secure AI Data Pipeline

## Purpose
This document describes how encryption is applied to protect data as it moves into and out of the AI/RAG system.

## Encryption Objectives
- Protect sensitive documents at rest in the knowledge base
- Protect data in transit between all components
- Maintain strong control over encryption keys
- Ensure only authorized identities can trigger decryption

## Encryption at Rest

### Approach
- All documents and embeddings stored in the knowledge base are encrypted using Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK).
- Encryption occurs before data is written to storage.
- The AI/RAG service does not store plaintext data persistently.

### Key Management
- Keys are managed in Cloud KMS (or equivalent).
- Keys are not exportable.
- Access to use the keys is granted only to specific service identities through IAM.
- Key administrators are separated from data access roles (separation of duties).
- Key rotation is enabled according to organizational policy.

### Recommended Key Strategy
| Data Type              | Key Type       | Notes                              |
|------------------------|----------------|------------------------------------|
| Knowledge base content | CMEK           | Primary data protection            |
| Embeddings             | Same or related CMEK | Can use same key for simplicity |
| Temporary processing   | Application-level or CMEK | Minimize plaintext lifetime     |

## Encryption in Transit
- All connections use TLS 1.2 or higher.
- This includes:
  - Ingestion → Encryption service
  - Encryption service ↔ KMS
  - AI service ↔ Knowledge base
  - AI service ↔ LLM provider
- Private Google Access / private networking is preferred where possible.

## Decryption Controls
- Decryption is only possible when an authorized identity makes a request through the proper access path.
- The AI service identity must have both:
  1. Permission to read the encrypted object
  2. Permission to use the relevant KMS key
- Decryption events are logged.

## Design Decisions
- Encrypt early (at ingestion) and decrypt late (only when needed by the AI component).
- Avoid long-term plaintext storage.
- Prefer customer-managed keys over default cloud provider keys for better control and auditability.
- Keep key access tightly scoped.

## Residual Risks
- Compromise of highly privileged KMS administrators
- Misconfigured IAM allowing broader key usage than intended
- Data decrypted into memory by the AI service could still be exposed through application-level vulnerabilities
